description:
@weecorbie I found that I have two more recordings from Yellowbank Wildlife Management Area along Highway 259 in Breckenridge County, KY. I initially got these recordings for the frogs but there's an abundance of cicada noise in the recordings. Changing the ID to help provide more data for cicadas in that area ( the frogs will still be singing at other times, the cicadas only show up every 17 years! ) 
Plotting location of observation manually so it's not exact. I know I was on Highway 259 and at the southern (or western) edge of that part of Yellowbank Wildlife Management Area property, it was ? the last place I could pull off the road and wander about a short distance. I think there was an old cabin there. Field habitat on my side of the road and across the road was a small pond and brushy area and then it looked like forested slope habitat.  Actually I think that was the edge of the forested habitat because when I left there it was farmland + small residential plots of land from there to where I picked up US 60 to head to Hancock County , KY. There was a thundershower with a downpour of rain about an hour earlier. I don't think cicadas were singing much when I initially arrived at the Yellowbank WMA zone but then they really started tuning up.  The sun came out for a while, then there was more rain in the area about 2-3 hours later. 
    Another note - I found an audio recording I still have on my Merlin app from the nearby Rhodelia area that afternoon ( Meade County, close to Breckenridge County line). I guess it's when I drove along 259 through Yellowbank and crossed back into Meade County and had to stop at a church parking lot to recheck my map & turn around, head back to Yellowbank.  Lots of cicada noise, I can tell Pharohs are in it, I don't know how to ID the other species but I think there's another species in the recording as well. I'll try to figure out how to get recording onto iNat for Magicicada data.

@weecorbie Is this the mark of Periodical Cicadas?
Otter Creek Recreational Area ( Meade County, KY), + Periodical Cicadas present two weeks ago and a few heard today.
I'm not sure if this is the "right" way to put it on iNaturalist and can delete. Just want to find out if that's what I should be looking for and if so get it reported appropriately

@weecorbie Evidence of egg deposition slits in an area where periodical cicadas were documented as present by sight & sound a few weeks ago.
Fishermans Park on Old Heady Rd near I-265 in southeastern Jefferson County, KY
Scattered trees at the park with clusters of brown leaves at the tips of branches + some littering the ground.  I was able to see slits in a few of the stems, so at least can confirm that some of the dead clusters of leaves show evidence suggesting it is due to cicadas
